description:
Implement the wire-level primitives the cert handshake needs, extending clients/typescript/src/proto.ts.

Two pieces:

1. protodelim framing — Go uses protodelim.MarshalTo/UnmarshalFrom which prefixes each protobuf message with a varint length. The current TS Stream.readFramed() handles WebSocket binary frames (one message per frame); cert messages are length-delimited inside a stream. Add a varint-length frame reader/writer that wraps the existing transport.

2. Hand-rolled protobuf encode/decode for the messages in api/cert.proto:
   - Cert { bytes content, bytes signature }
   - SignedCertContent { string agent_id, bytes peer_pubkey, string label, uint32 caps_bits, Timestamp not_before, Timestamp not_after, string serial, string workspace_id }
   - CertHandshakeMessage { oneof payload { CertHello hello = 1, SignedNonce proof = 2 } }
   - CertHello { Cert cert, bytes nonce }
   - SignedNonce { bytes signature }

Existing proto.ts already hand-rolls the auth messages (no protobuf runtime), so stay consistent — keep zero protobuf dependencies. Make encoding deterministic (matches Go's deterministic=true used at signing time).

Note: SignedCertContent is signed by the master; the bytes that are signed are Cert.content, NOT a re-serialization. So we never need to re-encode SignedCertContent for verification — only decode it after the signature passes.
